#: ../../source/randexp_README.md:53
msgid "Motivation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/randexp_README.md:55
msgid ""
"Regular expressions are used in every language, every programmer is "
"familiar with them. Regex can be used to easily express complex strings. "
"What better way to generate a random string than with a language you can "
"use to express the string you want?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/randexp_README.md:57
msgid ""
"Thanks to [String-Random](http://search.cpan.org/~steve/String-"
"Random-0.22/lib/String/Random.pm) for giving me the idea to make this in "
"the first place and [randexp](https://github.com/benburkert/randexp) for "
"the sweet `.gen()` syntax."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/randexp_README.md:59
msgid "Default Range"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/randexp_README.md:61
msgid ""
"The default generated character range includes printable ASCII.  In order"
" to add or remove characters, a `defaultRange` attribute is exposed. you "
"can `subtract(from, to)` and `add(from, to)`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/randexp_README.md:71
msgid "Custom PRNG"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/randexp_README.md:73
msgid ""
"The default randomness is provided by `Math.random()`. If you need to use"
" a seedable or cryptographic PRNG, you can override "
"`RandExp.prototype.randInt` or `randexp.randInt` (where `randexp` is an "
"instance of `RandExp`). `randInt(from, to)` accepts an inclusive range "
"and returns a randomly selected number within that range."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/randexp_README.md:77
msgid "Infinite Repetitionals"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/randexp_README.md:79
#, python-brace-format
msgid ""
"Repetitional tokens such as `*`, `+`, and `{3,}` have an infinite max "
"range. In this case, randexp looks at its min and adds 100 to it to get a"
" useable max value. If you want to use another int other than 100 you can"
" change the `max` property in `RandExp.prototype` or the RandExp "
"instance."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/randexp_README.md:86
msgid "With `RandExp.sugar()`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/randexp_README.md:93
msgid "Bad Regular Expressions"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/randexp_README.md:95
msgid "There are some regular expressions which can never match any string."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/randexp_README.md:97
msgid ""
"Ones with badly placed positionals such as `/a^/` and `/$c/m`. Randexp "
"will ignore positional tokens."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/randexp_README.md:99
msgid ""
"Back references to non-existing groups like `/(a)\\1\\2/`. Randexp will "
"ignore those references, returning an empty string for them. If the group"
" exists only after the reference is used such as in `/\\1 (hey)/`, it "
"will too be ignored."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/randexp_README.md:101
msgid ""
"Custom negated character sets with two sets inside that cancel each other"
" out. Example: `/[^\\w\\W]/`. If you give this to randexp, it will return"
" an empty string for this set since it can't match anything."
msgstr ""

